```java
package org.techhub;

public class Player {

    private int id;
    private String name;
    private int run;

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getRun() {
        return run;
    }

    public void setRun(int run) {
        this.run = run;
    }
}
```

---

## Theory

The `Player` class is called a **POJO (Plain Old Java Object)** because:

* It contains private variables.
* It provides public getter and setter methods.
* It does not depend on Spring APIs.

The values of `id`, `name`, and `run` will be injected using setter methods.

