# 1. Setter Injection / Setter Dependency Injection

Setter Injection means **Spring container injects values into bean properties using setter methods**.

Spring calls setter methods automatically.

### Example

## Bean Class (POJO)

```java
package org.techhub;

public class Player {
    private int id;
    private String name;
    private int run;

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getRun() {
        return run;
    }

    public void setRun(int run) {
        this.run = run;
    }
}
```

---

## XML Configuration (player.xml)

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE beans PUBLIC "-//SPRING//DTD BEAN 2.0//EN"
        "https://www.springframework.org/dtd/spring-beans-2.0.dtd">

<beans>
    <bean id="p" class="org.techhub.Player">
        <property name="id" value="1"/>
        <property name="name" value="Virat"/>
        <property name="run" value="12000"/>
    </bean>
</beans>
```

Here Spring internally calls:

```java
player.setId(1);
player.setName("Virat");
player.setRun(12000);
```

---

## Client Application

```java
package org.techhub;

import org.springframework.context.support.ClassPathXmlApplicationContext;

public class PlayerClientApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ClassPathXmlApplicationContext context =
                new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("player.xml");

        Player player = (Player) context.getBean("p");

        System.out.println(
                player.getId() + "\t" +
                player.getName() + "\t" +
                player.getRun()
        );

        context.close();
    }
}
```

### Output

```java
